  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2712"
 },
 "111": {
  "number": 111,
  "byline_raw": "Basile, Giacomo Antonio — Naples, February 24, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 248",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "With a lively hope and an inward sentiment I feel moved today, which is the feast of Saint Matthias, to write to Your Paternity on a matter of election, since no good fortune can be hoped for but where Saint Matthias enters as advocate, to whom so happy a lot once fell. Your Paternity knows the desire which God of His grace has granted me, to go and serve Him as His laborer in the Indies; and I do not doubt in the least that the Lord has destined me to be a missioner in those parts, since He never ceases to call me and invite me continually. Now I have spoken here with the Father Procurator of Portugal, whom I understand must be provided with a companion as far as Portugal. I have offered myself to him, even as a servant; and His Reverence does not refuse me, but with sincere affection refers everything to the election of Your Paternity, to whom he tells me he will write, and whom the Father Assistant of Portugal will also beseech in my favor. So I can now say to Your Paternity, whom I hold in the place of God: In manibus tuis sortes meae, Ostende quem elegeris. Ecce me, Pater, ad omnia paratum (in thy hands are my lots; show whom thou hast chosen. Behold me, Father, ready for all things). If Your Paternity has so often consoled and enkindled me with good hopes, let good fortune now console me upon myself. I, prostrate with all affection before the feet of Your Paternity, beg in Visceribus Christi (in the heart of Christ) to grant me this grace: to give me as companion to the Father Procurator of Portugal, from whence I may then depart for the Indies at the first embarkation. I hope in my Lord Jesus Christ that He will inspire this thought in Your Paternity, while I remain meanwhile with all humility awaiting from His hands my blessed lot. And I commend myself most humbly to His holy Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy son and servant in Christ — Giacomo Antonio Basile. Naples, 24 February 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Basile, Giacomo Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, February 24, 1640",
  "date_only": "February 24, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 24,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 157,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2724"
 },
 "112": {
  "number": 112,
  "byline_raw": "Albergati, Federico — Faenza, April 3, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 264",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I conveyed to Your Paternity, in two letters of mine sent to him by Father Fabio a few months ago, the sentiments and desires which it pleased His Divine Majesty to give me concerning the Mission of the Indies — for His honor, in penance for my sins, to repay reasonably with my blood the great harm done to the Holy Company, and the bad example received for so long from the world, to the prejudice of so many souls redeemed with the precious blood of the Lord. Now that I am well inspired by His Divine Majesty in these holy days to confirm the same to him with this other letter of mine which I send him immediately, I recommend myself with all affection and humility to his devout Prayers, assuring him that I am most ready, with the help of the Lord, at every nod of his to betake myself wherever it shall seem good and for the service of His Divine Majesty — for I am truly of little worth, and rather crude; yet grounding myself well in Christian Doctrine and in the principal things of the Holy Faith, with a little good will and the holy fear of God, I hope it will suffice, since it is not necessary that in those lands all be theologians. And for the end, I humbly reverence Your Paternity, and sincerely beg his blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy son and servant in the Lord — Federico Albergati. Faenza, 3 April 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Albergati, Federico",
  "place_raw": "Faenza",
  "date_raw": "Faenza, April 3, 1640",
  "date_only": "April 3, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 3,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 159,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2718"
 },
 "113": {
  "number": 113,
  "byline_raw": "Vimercati, Orazio — Cremona, May 5, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 272",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Now that I find myself, by the grace of the Lord, at the end both of my studies and of my fifth year of school, I come anew to beseech Your Paternity for the grace for which I have beseeched him at other times, both by letters and in person — that is, the Mission to the Indies. Let Your Paternity believe me, for I confess it to him before the Lord: the voice of God, which calls me thither, has never ceased in me; and I am by now certain that nothing moves me but the pure desire of serving Him in those parts, and of leaving for His love whatever contentment I might have in these. Such has always been my thought from the beginning of my entrance into Religion. I therefore pray Your Paternity with all humility that he would, with his blessing, grant my petition, to which I shall always hope in the Lord to be able to correspond, since He makes me so determinedly propose it to myself. I am no longer fixed upon one place rather than another; I place myself entirely in his paternal hands, and I shall hold myself fully satisfied with whichever place I am assigned. Hoping for which, I humbly reverence him and commend myself with all my heart to his holy Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy servant and son in Christ — Orazio Vimercati. Cremona, 5 May 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Vimercati, Orazio",
  "place_raw": "Cremona",
  "date_raw": "Cremona, May 5, 1640",
  "date_only": "May 5, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 160,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2708"
 },
 "114": {
  "number": 114,
  "byline_raw": "Buscemi, Tomaso — Piazza, May 7, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 274",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Upon hearing read aloud the glorious end of our Father Marcello Mastrilli, the Lord deigned to stir in my soul a most ardent desire to be his imitator, employing myself for the spiritual benefit of the Gentiles; and having commended this desire of mine repeatedly and most fervently to God our Lord, and conferred about it with spiritual Fathers, I have resolved to make it known to Your Paternity, so that on the occasions that present themselves he might make use of me, and console me with this employment so desired by me. At present, having finished studying in the Academy, I am teaching school, and next year, according to the plan of the Father Provincial, I shall begin Philosophy. I know well that many have been knocking at this door, and for a long time; but I, though the last, hope to be numbered among the first, for the fervor which the Divine Majesty makes me feel in my breast gives me courage for it. To Your Paternity I commend myself heartily, as a most unworthy son, and I pray him to remember me in his holy Sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's servant in the Lord — Tomaso Buscemi. From Piazza, 7 May 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Buscemi, Tomaso",
  "place_raw": "Piazza",
  "date_raw": "Piazza, May 7, 1640",
  "date_only": "May 7, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 7,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 161,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2715"
 },
 "115": {
  "number": 115,
  "byline_raw": "Arganantio, Domenico — Noto, May 17, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 281",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I received Your Paternity's letter of the 5th of April, which filled my soul to the brim with unspeakable joy, to see that the memory of me, a most vile sinner, is so alive before him. Truly all of this must be attributed to the great charity of so loving a Father, and not to the merits of so miserable a son, and unworthy of every good. Confiding therefore in the great benignity of Your Paternity, I do not weary of multiplying letters to lay open the ardent and kindled desires of my heart concerning the mission of the Indies; nor does the lack of an occasion at present hinder me at all in my wonted desire — rather, it goes on being fomented with still greater increase, for it is most true what is commonly said, that privation most often generates a greater appetite for things. For the rest, I will not multiply words further, for Your Paternity now possesses entirely the whole interior of my heart. In the meantime I shall attend, as Your Paternity exhorts me, to making continual prayer to God our Lord concerning this matter, that He would direct the affair to His greater glory, the good of my soul, and the salvation of our neighbors — for all this is my end, nor do I pretend to anything else in this petition; and I stand awaiting from the hand of God whatever shall be determined concerning it, conforming myself in all things to the divine good pleasure. Finally, our Father, I, as a most unworthy son, most humbly bow and ask from Your Paternity his holy blessing, commending myself with all my heart to his most holy Sacrifices and devout Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son and servant — Domenico Arganantio. Noto, 17 May 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Arganantio, Domenico",
  "place_raw": "Noto",
  "date_raw": "Noto, May 17, 1640",
  "date_only": "May 17, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 17,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 162,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2725"
 },
 "116": {
  "number": 116,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, May 27, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 288",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write anew to Your Paternity concerning my Vocation to Japan, beseeching him with the most heartfelt affection of my soul to have compassion on a son of his in Christ in so pious a matter; and let him know that I shall never be forgetful of so great a benefit, for me unique and singular. And though the cause may perhaps be the lack of opportunity — the Procurator of China not having come — yet I cannot be quieted, knowing that Your Paternity, this notwithstanding, can console me, as he has just now done with certain others; for which I cannot but afflict myself with holy envy. Of grace, let him console me, for I am all too distressed by it, feeling myself inwardly reproved as of little worth and unmeriting. At least let him promise me that among the first who shall be called, I too shall be; for I know no other remedy to quiet myself a little than this. Father, as I write this, tears of tenderness come to me, nor do I know how to express my heart to him; and this has become for me a solicitude and a most sorrowful anxiety. I end, to trouble him no further, only reminding him that I am utterly, utterly disconsolate and afflicted in my heart, thinking of nothing else but this; beseeching the Most Blessed Virgin that she would deign at last to move Your Paternity to my just prayers; and offering myself to him most ready, I commend myself in his most holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy servant and son in Christ — Mario Finechiaro. From Palermo, 27 May 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, May 27, 1640",
  "date_only": "May 27, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 27,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 164,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2754"
 },
 "117": {
  "number": 117,
  "byline_raw": "Barberito, Felice — Massa, May 29, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 294",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The ardent desire which the Lord once deigned to awaken in my breast, to go to the most needy and perilous parts of the Indies for the increase of His Holy Faith, He has deigned also to preserve in me until now — as I hope He will preserve it for me until I have attained my aim. Wherefore, as your unworthy son in the Lord, I come anew with this letter to wish Your Paternity prosperous and happy these holy feasts of Pentecost, and to pray him humbly with all the affection I can, that as soon as possible he would fulfill for me so holy and pious a desire; for in no other thing could I find, in the Holy Company, the satisfaction I shall find in aiding souls and in passing all my life in this single exercise — which I hope to obtain from the same One who, of His mercy, deigned to give me such a will. Finally, I ask his paternal blessing, doing him most humble reverence, and commending myself to his holy Sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Felice Barberito. Massa, 29 May 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barberito, Felice",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, May 29, 1640",
  "date_only": "May 29, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 165,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2711"
 },
 "118": {
  "number": 118,
  "byline_raw": "Arcamone, Ignatio — Bari, June 9, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 303",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I recall my desire for the India mission, now that Your Paternity is about to send subjects to China. I find myself finished with the studies of philosophy, in my third year of school; I would pray Your Paternity to send me on the next mission, dispensing me from the fourth year of school, since they dispense my classmates only for entering Theology. For the rest, I am ready all my life to teach school and every other exercise, should the so-desired India mission fail me through my unfitness. Which mission I presently beg of Your Paternity, being entirely disengaged from my family, which has no need of my person; and I stand confident in the Divine omnipotence which calls me thither, all the more since it has not left me so incapable of learning foreign languages. And commending myself to his holy sacrifices, I await the Divine will from Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy son in the Lord — Ignatio Arcamone. From Bari, 9 June 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Arcamone, Ignatio",
  "place_raw": "Bari",
  "date_raw": "Bari, June 9, 1640",
  "date_only": "June 9, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 6,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 166,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2717"
 },
 "119": {
  "number": 119,
  "byline_raw": "Arcolino, Domenico — Vicenza, July 5, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 329",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Behold, the hour has come for me to keep the promises made so long ago to my God. My Lord wants me; I cannot say no. I am forced to petition Your Paternity to put into execution the Vow already granted me, of the India mission. The spurs are too sharp. So many peoples, who petunt panem et nemo est qui frangat eis (they seek bread, and there is none to break it for them), call me incessantly. The love of my God urges me greatly; either I must run where I am called, or I put myself in danger of losing the favors which my Lord has hitherto lavished upon me, for Bonorum meorum non eget (he has no need of my goods). Ah, most loving Father, do not prolong my going; the occasion is at hand. The laborers requested, I think, are many; the most abundant harvest, I doubt not, far exceeds their strength. Choose me, who though the least of all, as in spirit and talents, yet in desires am not less, if not greater. The petition I make of him is such that it far exceeds the past ones. I would have wished to show it by imprinting on this sheet characters formed of my own blood, had Obedience not forbidden it. Ah, dearest Father, satisfy a son who asks of him so licit a thing. Look not, I pray, upon my failings, which I confess to have been many and grave, but recognized with sorrow and repentance; rather, as penance for them I choose to depart from beautiful Italy, to take voluntary exile from relatives and friends, to go among strange or barbarian peoples, to plunge into oceans of sufferings, to end my life on the scaffold. I set before Your Paternity only the most copious sweats shed by my great Father and Apostle Saint Francis Xavier, the precious blood of so many of our holy Martyrs, which call and invite me to the cultivation of those souls so dearly bought by most sweet JESUS, that I beg them to satisfy my great thirst; which I assure him, that of every drop of sweat or blood which shall flow from me, Your Paternity shall always have a share — Qua[m] Deus (which God) (the letter breaks off here)."
  ],
  "signoff": [],
  "editorial_notes": [
   "(Letter incomplete as preserved.) Received 5 July 1640 — Domenico Arcolino."
  ],
  "author_raw": "Arcolino, Domenico",
  "place_raw": "Vicenza",
  "date_raw": "Vicenza, July 5, 1640",
  "date_only": "July 5, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 167,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2726"
 },
 "120": {
  "number": 120,
  "byline_raw": "Arisco, Lorenzo — Caltagirone, July 13, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 336",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "On the first of last Lent I wrote to Your Paternity a letter in which I disclosed to him the great desire which God our Lord has deigned to give me ever since my novitiate, of serving Him in the Indies, offering myself to Your Paternity — that if he had need there of coadjutor brothers, I, who by the grace of the Lord am young and have strength, was most ready to go thither. With this I again beseech Your Paternity, for the love he may have for Jesus Christ, that he would grant my petition, and do me the charity of sending me to those parts as soon as possible; for as for me, on the one hand I am indifferent to whatever exercise the superiors there shall apply me to, be it two or three; on the other hand, here I have no attachment to anything that might impede my departure — rather, I shall receive the notice to depart, when Your Paternity shall do me the charity of calling me, as if it were an angel calling me to the glory of Paradise, for to labor for the love of God and to serve my Fathers, who aid those poor souls redeemed with the most precious blood of the Son of God, is to me a Paradise, and I do not hold it inferior to glory — which I pray God our Lord to grant to Your Paternity, to me, to all of our Holy Company, and to all the world. With which I commend myself to his holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy servant and son — Lorenzo Arisco. From Caltagirone, 13 July 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Arisco, Lorenzo",
  "place_raw": "Caltagirone",
  "date_raw": "Caltagirone, July 13, 1640",
  "date_only": "July 13, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 13,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 169,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2714"
 },
 "121": {
  "number": 121,
  "byline_raw": "Buscemi, Tomaso — Palermo, July 26, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 348",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I had thought, having a short while ago received from Your Paternity, on the last of May, a letter most welcome to me, that I should not so often weary you with new letters; yet I confess candidly that the ardent desire that dwells within my breast can endure no delay, nor let any occasion pass for obtaining what I so long for. When? My Father, when? shall it be that I see myself immersed in a thousand labors for love of my beloved Jesus; and, already cast away upon some shore of Japan, abandoned and deprived of human aid, breathe my last sigh to the Creator — for I dare not promise myself martyrdom, which was so utterly denied to my glorious Saint Francis; yet if divine goodness be pleased to grant it to me in recompense for my many sins, I embrace it with good will. It seems to me, to tell the truth, that I dwell in our own lands as in a prison, while my heart by night and by day journeys through those of Japan, panting for labor and suffering. Ah, for grace, my Father, by the bowels of the pity of Christ Jesus, by His sweet side, for love of the most holy Virgin, to whom just now I am about to dispatch a letter, in imitation of so many sacrifices, albeit most unworthily, in which I most fervently ask the same — let Your Paternity console this poor man. Already I hear that the Father Procurator of the Philippines has arrived, and that the Procurator of China is expected: what more is lacking? I cannot satisfy my affection, for it would never end; enough — may that Father of mercies, who, calling me from the world, drew me from a dunghill of sins in which I miserably lay, and who now gives me so fervent a vocation for Japan, efficaciously move Your Paternity to grant me the grace as soon as possible — and so humbly I do you reverence."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s servant in Christ — Thomaso Busciemi. Palermo, 26 July 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Buscemi, Tomaso",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, July 26, 1640",
  "date_only": "July 26, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 26,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 170,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2716"
 },
 "122": {
  "number": 122,
  "byline_raw": "Barbarito, Felice — Massa, July 31, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 352",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "From the depths of my heart, with most ardent desire, I write to Your Paternity, entreating you anew with the greatest affection I can, for the glorious mission of the Indies; for I hold — which is the highest benefit of His Divine Majesty — that I cannot live content, nor with true spirit, in this holy Company, unless I employ myself wholly in the salvation of souls, for which I entered it; and this I can nowhere better accomplish than in the Indies, where I hold it certain that I must one day, with the aid of Him who deigned to communicate to me so holy a desire, arrive. My Father, anew, with the greatest reverence, confidence, and affection, I beg you: since the Lord from Heaven has for a long time — as Your Paternity knows — deigned to infuse into me, His most unworthy creature, this so holy desire of going to the Indies, let not Your Paternity on earth hinder me, though I be most unworthy, being your son in the Lord; and with this I do you most humble reverence, asking your paternal blessing, and commending myself to your holy sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s humble servant and son in the Lord — Felice Barbarito. Massa, the glorious day of our holy Father Ignatius, 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barbarito, Felice",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, July 31, 1640",
  "date_only": "July 31, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 31,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 172,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2710"
 },
 "123": {
  "number": 123,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, September 9, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 372",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I am aware that it is not necessary to commend to Your Paternity so often my desire for the Japan mission, believing that you cannot so easily have forgotten my long insistence and fervor: yet today I feel myself inwardly moved to lay before you the state of this vocation of mine, shown to you so many, many times; knowing especially that if Your Paternity will take pity on this your most unworthy son in Christ, you can console him — for among so many, I too, poor wretch, might well find a place. So I pray you in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ) to console me at least by giving me sure hope, that I may in part mitigate the ardor; which has grown so keen from day to day that, knowing not what else to do, I have promised it by vow to the Divine Majesty, with the permission of my Superiors; whence, seeing that it is not yet answered, I feel myself afflicted, and often cannot restrain myself from breaking out in copious weeping. I finish, lest I weary you, and offering myself to you, commend myself in your holy sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s most unworthy servant and son in Christ — Mario Finechiaro. Palermo, 9 September 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 9, 1640",
  "date_only": "September 9, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 173,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2755"
 },
 "124": {
  "number": 124,
  "byline_raw": "Barbarito, Giovanni Antonio — San [Solio], September 29, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 382",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "This very morning I heard here at San [Solio], where I find myself for the vacation, that Your Paternity has already sent to some the most happy news for the Indies — among whom I have not seen myself numbered. I confess that I am the most unworthy of all, which I say most sincerely; but I pray Your Paternity that if others of this Province are to be sent — which I hope without doubt — she send among them also me, poor wretch, so that I may be made worthy to spend the little life that remains to me in the service of that Lord whom I have so many times offended. Our Father, I recognize myself bound to blessed God in extraordinary measure, and perhaps more than any other, for the great perils from which He has delivered me; and precisely today, the glorious day of Saint Michael the Archangel, twelve years ago, He delivered me from a perilous accident of my life — for, tumbling from a steep rock some fifty-seven palms above the ground, I received no injury from the fall, though I ought to have perished, as those who saw the precipice judged before I reached the ground. So let Your Paternity be content, for the love of blessed God, that I may spend these few days, which in His supreme goodness He has deigned to prolong for me, there in the Indies in aid of those poor souls, bereft of the light of the holy faith. The Father Provincial long ago promised me that he would not hinder me on account of the school, about which he had informed me. So I shall await this good news from Your Paternity, and I shall pray blessed God in the Spiritual Exercises, which I am about to begin as soon as possible, to deign to inspire Your Paternity to grant it to me. And with this, making to Your Paternity most humble reverence, I pray you give me your holy blessing."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s most humble servant and most obedient son — Giovanni Antonio Barbarito. San [Solio], 29 September 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Barbarito, Giovanni Antonio",
  "place_raw": "San [Solio]",
  "date_raw": "San [Solio], September 29, 1640",
  "date_only": "September 29, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 174,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2727"
 },
 "125": {
  "number": 125,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, October 1, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 383",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I think that by now, by God’s grace, the Father Procurator of China has arrived there; wherefore I pray with the warmest affection of my heart Your Paternity to remember this your unworthy son in Christ. Our Father, now is the time at last to console me, and among so many I too may well find a place — you know how long I have desired and sighed for this; and I have taken such affection to this mission of Japan that I would have all ask for it, and would desire all to employ themselves wholly in aid of those poor souls redeemed with the blood of my Jesus; yet, I confess to Your Paternity the whole truth candidly, I have been exceedingly afflicted — even shedding copious tears before the Crucifix — at seeing the ardent promises, made here by many grave Fathers, to some, of being fully consoled; for I too would fain have now some like consolation; yet I claim it from no other than from the paternal charity of Your Paternity, from whom I instantly ask that you confirm to me the vow which I have made of this with the permission of my Father Rector in this secular year; for I feel myself consumed with the desire of offering my whole self to my Lord, and while it is granted me to fulfill it, I esteem it my greatest consolation to offer myself in vow with such a holocaust; and offering myself to you, I commend myself in the holy sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s most unworthy servant and son in Christ — Mario Finechiaro. Palermo, 1 October 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, October 1, 1640",
  "date_only": "October 1, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 1,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 176,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2756"
 },
 "126": {
  "number": 126,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Palermo, October 14, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 390",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The news that comes here of the calling to the Indies of some from the Province of Naples, though on the one hand it has brought me the greatest consolation, as I rejoice in their joy and in the aid they will give to that nascent Church, yet on the other, in not seeing myself yet called, has caused me so great an affliction that I am compelled by grief to inform Your Paternity of it, and to pray you in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ) to have pity and compassion on me — for you know how ardent and fervent my longing for the Japan mission has ever been. Our Father, the solicitude of this business so consumes me that I know not what more to do, seeing I cannot pray you more effectively than I have always done. For the rest, I place myself wholly in the paternal hands of Your Paternity, who, I know, will not consent that I remain afflicted as I now am; nor do I ask anything else of you but that you give me at least some sure hope for the quiet of my heart — for truly I am much, much troubled. This being for nothing else, I commend myself in the holy sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s most unworthy servant and son in Christ — Mario Finechiaro. Palermo, 14 October 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, October 14, 1640",
  "date_only": "October 14, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 14,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 177,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2757"
 },
 "127": {
  "number": 127,
  "byline_raw": "Arisco, Lorenzo — Caltagirone, October 28, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 394",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Seeing that from our College there departs for the Indies, at Your Paternity’s command, Giuseppe Plazza, and hearing that many will go, has kindled a flame in my heart that will not let me rest. It is true that I am a poor brother; but I was healed of a malignant fever by a saint whom I did not know at the time and of whom I had never heard word — and then, having entered the Company, I came to know that the saint I saw in a dream was Saint Xavier. Now he calls me to the Indies, and I give notice of it to Your Paternity and supplicate you, as I have done at other times. Here I cannot labor as I would, for though I do all the offices and services as I know how and can — and have often done, amid the murmurings of the brothers — the superiors restrain me; but in the Indies one man will need to do all things. If there be difficulty about the expense, I will bear it at least in part. For the love of God and of Saint Xavier, who healed me and made me enter the Company, let Your Paternity console me, and at once; and afterward I shall report to Your Paternity all that I shall suffer and do in the Indies, for the service of God and of the Fathers and brothers; at the most holy sacrifices and prayers I commend myself to Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s servant in Christ — Lorenzo Arisco. Caltagirone, 28 October 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Arisco, Lorenzo",
  "place_raw": "Caltagirone",
  "date_raw": "Caltagirone, October 28, 1640",
  "date_only": "October 28, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 28,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 178,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2713"
 },
 "128": {
  "number": 128,
  "byline_raw": "Aliotta, Ignazio — Trapani, December 18, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 420",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Would to the Lord that my pen could answer in kind to what I now feel interiorly communicated to me by that infinite Mercy, and set forth to you effectively the desire I have to see myself employed by Your Paternity in some part, either of the Indies or of any other country whatsoever, where the greater service of God might be judged to lie — for the conversion of souls, to which I would devote all my strength, shedding even my own blood, if such were the pleasure of that Divine Goodness, to which I recognize myself in particular so indebted for the infinite graces I have received and continually receive. And although this desire has been ever fixed in my heart — for such sentiments did not fail me six years ago, when I was a philosopher, and for my part it was not lacking that I represented them to Your Paternity — yet it concerns me to render obedience to the spiritual Fathers, who with better judgment and experience of things judged for the time that I should make no show of it, until I had first certified myself of the soundness of this desire and were not moved to it by mere childish sentiment. Now, seeing myself of some years, a theologian of the second year, it seems to me that I can form a judgment, and well understand what I ask of Your Paternity — the more so now, for the good news that comes from those parts, desirous of men, stirs me in extraordinary fashion — so that, in keeping with the promises made to the Lord, I cannot but, not once but again and again, instantly supplicate Your Paternity to place me in the number of those destined for such missions; and I hope in divine pity that Your Paternity’s heart will be moved to place me among those who shall go soonest — for certainly I could not but shed tears if I saw myself left behind by them, so great is my desire to follow them. And let Your Paternity believe me, that I would not declare myself to you in this manner were I not wholly resolved — for I have no other consolation but this: that, by the grace of the Lord, one day I shall see myself far off, journeying toward the Indies for the conversion of those souls; unless my many sins hinder me, for which not so great a grace, but rather great chastisements, I ought to expect. I pray the Lord to keep me with this desire, giving me spirit to serve Him in such an enterprise, and to Your Paternity a particular sentiment to console me — which I the more promise myself and hope for, confiding in your merits and prayers, to which I commend myself with all my heart."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s servant and son — Ignatio Aliotta. Trapani, 18 December 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Aliotta, Ignazio",
  "place_raw": "Trapani",
  "date_raw": "Trapani, December 18, 1640",
  "date_only": "December 18, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 18,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 179,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2709"
 },
 "129": {
  "number": 129,
  "byline_raw": "Basile, Giacomo Antonio — Naples, December 29, 1640",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 742, 424",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The scruple of not wishing to be indiscreet by multiplying letters to Your Paternity more often on the same matter has sufficed to restrain me for some three months from writing to you, and from renewing the memory of the holy desires which God of His grace preserves in me, for the Indies. But to defer longer would not have freed me from the fault of negligence. The present feasts of the most holy Christmas, and the approaching renewal of spirit for which we are preparing, are a most urgent occasion to stir in me new fervor and desire — for so great a Lord, for us made man. And I, knowing by experience how much I have ever been aided in this by communicating my desires to Your Paternity, and by your answers, come in this to recall to you my ancient and continual desire to correspond to the most sweet call of God — I desire to employ myself with all promptness and indifference in the aid of souls in whatsoever part of the world the Lord shall inspire in Your Paternity, to whose determination I remain in all and for all committed at every moment. Meanwhile I humbly pray Your Paternity to commend me often to His Divine Majesty in your holy sacrifices, and to obtain for me the grace of corresponding punctually to His inspirations. And I too remain, praying from Heaven for Your Paternity many more new years and good feasts, for the common good of the whole Company.",
   "[Archival docket on the letter: Naples, 29 December 1640 — Father Giacomo Antonio Basile — Indies again.]"
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity’s most obedient son in Christ and servant — Giacomo Antonio Basile. Naples, 29 December 1640."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Basile, Giacomo Antonio",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, December 29, 1640",
  "date_only": "December 29, 1640",
  "year": 1640,
  "month": 12,
  "day": 29,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 181,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2728"
 },
 "130": {
  "number": 130,
  "byline_raw": "Archinto, Cristoforo — 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 33",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Never has the goodness of God failed to grant me His graces; rather, each day more He has increased them to me; wherefore I should judge myself guilty if I did not strive to correspond, as far as I can, to the desire He gives me to carry out the institute which I hope to take, unless hindered by my faults — especially as He gives me heart that I must do it in the parts of the Indies, where I have always had my aim, though not yet received into the Company, as I hinted to Your Paternity on my entrance into the novitiate. Now, feeling myself more than usually inflamed to do this, I supplicate Your Paternity to deign to carry out this desire which God gives me, with your command or permission — looking not at my many demerits, but only believing this to be the will of God, from whom I hope sufficient helps — and I shall remain under perpetual obligation to Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your most reverend Paternity’s most unworthy and most useless son and servant in the Lord — Christofforo Archinto, novice. 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Archinto, Cristoforo",
  "place_raw": null,
  "date_raw": "1641",
  "date_only": "1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": null,
  "day": null,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 183,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2680"
 },
 "131": {
  "number": 131,
  "byline_raw": "Lubelli, Francesco — Massa, March 12, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 27",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "On the occasion of the holy feasts of Easter, I come to wish you these, and many more in the time to come, all most happy in the Lord. Then, having cast myself at your feet, I come anew to ask of you the so-desired grace of going to Japan, or to whatever part of the lands of the Infidels, that I may satisfy the obligation I have to obey the call of the Lord: as Your Paternity knows. Yet I pray Your Paternity not to forget me when occasions arise, believing it to be the will of God, since with this desire and vocation He made me enter the Society. Still, I place myself in the hands of Your Paternity; with this, I pray you to keep memory of me in your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers, not failing also to beseech you to make me worthy of your holy blessing; and for the rest, offering you my most humble reverence."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy servant and son in Christ — Francesco Lubelli. Massa, 12 March 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lubelli, Francesco",
  "place_raw": "Massa",
  "date_raw": "Massa, March 12, 1641",
  "date_only": "March 12, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 3,
  "day": 12,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 184,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2758"
 },
 "132": {
  "number": 132,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, July 13, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 97",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I return to Your Paternity to make once more my petition for the Indies, and to recall to you my ardent desires, continued for so long a time. Already the Father Procurator of China finds himself in Rome, and I find myself disengaged from every other occupation by the particular providence of God, who wishes in this manner to show that He has kept me for the East; nothing remains to me but the blessing of Your Paternity, with which to set myself on the journey — of which he wished to give me a pledge a year and a half ago by sending me the Crucifix of the Indies.",
   "I pray His Divine Majesty that He may show to you the tears I shed, and the continual sighs for this end, and the prayers that each day I make to God, that they may move your heart to grant me the grace; and I hope that after so long a time you will grant it to me — at least propter importunitatem (on account of importunity). For this importunity I ask your pardon; yet I believe it is welcome to you, since it concerns a matter of such great service and glory of God. With this, praying you to give me your blessing, I end."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most useless servant and son in Christ Jesus — Leonardo Cinami. Naples, 13 July 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, July 13, 1641",
  "date_only": "July 13, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 13,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 185,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2850"
 },
 "133": {
  "number": 133,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, July 22, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 109",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Our Father, I cannot resist the fervor that the Lord gives me, nor does the thought that I give trouble to Your Paternity hold me back from writing; have patience, for the love of the Lord, and forgive, with your fatherly charity, one of your sons, unworthy otherwise of every good. To the vow that, with the license of the Father Provincial and the counsel of my spiritual father, Father Geronimo Tagliavia, I made to go and suffer for the love of God in those parts of the Indies, or in whatever other part, I have added this other: to ask of Your Paternity, for all the time of my life, with every efficacy and fervor, the execution, by me so longed for, of this matter; and the one and the other vow I would have sent to Your Paternity written in my own blood, as I keep it by me, had I not judged that to do so was a thing superfluous. Our Father, I know not what will become of me — Your Paternity, be moved to pity by my tears and prayers, and give me this happy news: that for the shed blood of Christ you are content that I go where the Lord calls me. O fortunate me, if I shall receive such a message! If Your Paternity asks it of me, I hope it will be so: the Lord gives me a living faith that will console me, as it will also cause Your Paternity, considering my failings washed in His most precious blood, not to let them hinder me — though I otherwise deserve it, for not having lived as a true son of the Society. In visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ), grant me this consolation. To the holy sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I greatly commend myself, and I ask of you your holy Benediction."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 22 July 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, July 22, 1641",
  "date_only": "July 22, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 7,
  "day": 22,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 186,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2787"
 },
 "134": {
  "number": 134,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, August 4, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 124",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "For the love of the Lord I pray with every insistence to Your Paternity that you pardon me the trouble I give you with so many letters, and forgive me, poor wretch that I am dying of the desire to be consoled by Your Paternity. Our Father, the Lord gives me such fervor, joined with such spurs, that I hold it for certain that if I spoke in person with Your Paternity, without doubt you would have compassion on me and would console me, granting me that which, since I entered the Society — fourteen years now — I have longed for; and Your Paternity, for your charity, for the space of eight years promised it to me, giving me firm hope in your letters that, should occasion arise, you would condescend to my request. The occasion has now come, so that it seems to me time to go where my heart is, and where the Lord calls me so ardently that I have no words to express it. O God, when shall that blessed hour be! O if Your Paternity could see the fire I have within my heart! I speak confidently, and as a son to a father. For the love of God, Our Father, console me; for I pray you, kneeling with tears in my eyes, and with the greatest affection that I know and can; and in case, by some accident — which the infinite mercy of God forbid — this mission of China should be deferred, Your Paternity at least do me the charity of giving me this happy news, of advising me from now for when it shall be. But I hope that the Lord, having pity on the tears of certain Indians who are in this College — who truly effundunt animas (they pour out their souls) in the sight of God — will remove every impediment, lest the Devil prevail in placing obstacles to so glorious an enterprise. I end by supplicating anew Your Paternity that you have pity on me. I am now learning the Spanish language. I ask of you your holy Benediction, and I commend myself to your holy Sacrifices and prayers."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 4 August 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, August 4, 1641",
  "date_only": "August 4, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 4,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 188,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2788"
 },
 "135": {
  "number": 135,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, August 12, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 130",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "It is not possible, Our Father, that I cease from writing; Your Paternity must lay the blame on the fervor that the Lord gives me, which is so great that I would go crying through the public streets begging the prayers of everyone, that God, of His infinite goodness, might be placated toward me, the greatest sinner, and inspire Your Paternity to grant me that which for many years I have longed for, and which by your fatherly charity was promised to me — and now, for desire, I feel my heart leaving me. O how I speak from the heart, Our Father! O that I were in Rome, that I might show you how I burn with desire, and what courage the Lord gives me to go and suffer and labor for His holy name! Our Father, I know not what to do; I would that my words were darts. For the love of Christ, console me, Your Paternity, and have compassion on this poor wretch, who thinks of nothing else but where the Lord calls him. To the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I greatly commend myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 12 August 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, August 12, 1641",
  "date_only": "August 12, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 12,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 190,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2789"
 },
 "136": {
  "number": 136,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, August 26, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 144",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I know full well that I trouble Your Paternity with my many letters, but trusting in your fatherly charity, and urged by the ardent desire I feel to go to those parts of the Indies where my heart is, I cannot do otherwise; affection forces me to knock continually at the door, until the divine goodness, of His infinite mercy, grants me the desired grace. O Our Father, how many times a day do I wish I were a bird, that flying I might transport myself to Rome to speak with Your Paternity, and plead my cause face to face! I know for certain that I would be consoled by obtaining that which now, and in these times with the coming of the Father Procurator of China, keeps me on tenterhooks, and all the day makes me sigh, and count the moments and the hours of the days until the post arrives. Believe me, Your Paternity, that I speak from the heart; rather, I affirm to you that my desire is greater than what I tell you, having no words, and finding none, that could express it. Give me [good] and fortunate news, Your Paternity, for the love of God, and do it moved by your fatherly charity, which is great toward me, your son — and toward me it has been very great. I ask of you your holy Benediction, and to the holy sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I greatly commend myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 26 August 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, August 26, 1641",
  "date_only": "August 26, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 26,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 191,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2790"
 },
 "137": {
  "number": 137,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, August 26, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 145",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The desire that by the grace of the Lord I have to go to those parts of the Infidels, and the double vow I have made to go to the Indies and to ask for the passage with every ardor, compel me to give Your Paternity frequent trouble with my letters. Have patience, for the love of the Lord, and console me, for the shed blood of Christ. Our Father, I know not what to do; I have no words to explain my desire. Believe me, Your Paternity, that I burn and am consumed with desire, and every moment seems to me a thousand years. O that I were in Rome! I count the hours of each day, of each week, until the post arrives, and always I believe that my consolation is coming; but my grave sins impede it. Yet I hope that the fortunate day will come when I shall be advised to set out for my Christ. Advise me soon, Your Paternity, for as much as you love the Most Holy Virgin, and pardon me if I speak in this manner, since the fervor — which in me is very great, by the mercy of God — carries me away. To the holy sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I commend myself from the heart."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 26 August 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, August 26, 1641",
  "date_only": "August 26, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 8,
  "day": 26,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 192,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2791"
 },
 "138": {
  "number": 138,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, September 7, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 165",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "O that within this letter I could send to Your Paternity my heart, fainting, rent in a thousand parts, that it might plead my cause and move to pity the paternal bowels of Your Paternity, to console me, a poor wretch, who burns and is consumed with desire to go to those parts of the Infidels to suffer for my God. I speak confidently, Our Father; Your Paternity, pardon the longing I feel, which is very great; and for the love he bears to the Most Holy Virgin — on the vigil of whose nativity I write this present — grant me this one consolation: to send me with Father Alvaro Semedo to those parts; and do it as punishment for my faults, in the same way that the gallows and exile are given as penalty to malefactors. O fortunate me, if I shall be punished in such a manner! Our Father, I know not more what to say to you, nor do I know how to explain to you the desire I feel within my heart. Hear me, Your Paternity, for your charity, and with the same pardon me the trouble I give you with my letters. I ask of you your holy Benediction, and to the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I greatly commend myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son in the Lord — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 9 September 1641 (the database dates this letter 7 September)."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 7, 1641",
  "date_only": "September 7, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 7,
  "date_note": "the database dates this letter 7 September",
  "book_page_start": 193,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2760"
 },
 "139": {
  "number": 139,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, September 7, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 161",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I have shown myself perhaps a little cold in writing, and in making new petitions to Your Paternity to obtain the grace of the Indies, because it seemed there was no certainty of the Mission; but now that so many have been chosen by Your Paternity, I weep for my negligence, in not having manifested my desire infinitely many times. I trust, however, in the extreme charity of Your Paternity that, with all this, you will not leave me behind, nor forget the petitions I have made at other times, nor the promises that, with the greatest benignity, Your Paternity has deigned to make me.",
   "My Father, the longing that God gives me to run to the East, and to expose myself to every torment for the glory of His Divine Majesty, is such that I {dare not} explain it in words; and then it has lasted for so long a time, with the same fervor, that it seems impossible it is not a thing of God. Your Paternity has had, for so many years, continual letters from me; I supplicate you to console me, and to send me there, where by so many signs, with so many calls, God wills that I go — and I would show you the tears that for this end I continually shed. I know not how to say more, except that this, on earth, is my only paradise; and so, with all the efficacy that I can, with my face to the ground, I pray you to grant me the grace; and I shall see myself bound, for all the time of my life, to pray always to God for Your Paternity — from whom I shall have received the greatest benefit that can be obtained on earth. With this, praying you to give me your blessing, I end."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most useless servant and son in Christ Jesus — Leonardo Cinami. Naples, 7 September 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, September 7, 1641",
  "date_only": "September 7, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 7,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 194,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2851"
 },
 "140": {
  "number": 140,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, September 9, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 166",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write this letter with blushing and shame, thinking of the annoyance I cause Your Paternity with my letters. But what of it? I trust in your paternal charity, which gives me heart to turn to you confidently and to hope for all that I long for; and with humble prayers I ask of Your Paternity nothing else but to go and suffer, and to labor in those parts of the Indies, where the Lord calls me through His infinite mercy. I no longer know what to say to Your Paternity: the desire I carry within my heart is immense, and it grants me no rest by day or by night, nor lets me think of anything else. For the love of the Lord, let Your Paternity do me the charity of consoling me with some happy and fortunate news. I ask your holy blessing, and I commend myself warmly to the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son in the Lord — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 9 September 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 9, 1641",
  "date_only": "September 9, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 9,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 196,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2761"
 },
 "141": {
  "number": 141,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, September 13, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 170",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I would be most perplexed and doubtful over the annoyance I cause Your Paternity with my letters, were it not that your paternal charity takes away all my fear — charity which I know for certain is not wearied by the importunity of its sons, and of mine in particular, which though great proceeds nonetheless from a heart that sighs night and day for the longed-for China, as God is my witness, He who sees the ardent desire I have to go and suffer in those parts for His holy name. Our Father, I do not know by what means to entreat Your Paternity to console me — O God! I have no more efficacious means than the most precious Blood of Christ, or the Most Holy Virgin: let Your Paternity do it for these two things, which I know are most dear to him. And just as a poor man asking alms for the Blood of Jesus and for the love of the Virgin is not refused, in the same way Your Paternity must not refuse me, who humbly and prostrate on the ground ask him for the Indies as alms, proposing to him the same means. My crucified Christ, grant that soon, soon, soon, through Your infinite mercy, I may be consoled. I ask your holy blessing, and I commend myself with all my heart to the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 13 September 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 13, 1641",
  "date_only": "September 13, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 13,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 197,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2762"
 },
 "142": {
  "number": 142,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, September 14, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 171",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "Before the reply can come, I add a second letter to Your Paternity, and on this occasion I beg you to grant me license to multiply them as many as I can, and perhaps to be importunate. Let Your Paternity alone read them; for the rest, I care for no other reply than the last, when I am notified. I return to making my usual petitions for the Indies, and I renew the tears that I have shed in so many other letters; nor shall I ever tire of writing while I have a hand, nor of writing and weeping while I have eyes, to obtain a thing which, once obtained, can bring me beatitude. I do not know how to explain my desire to you further, except by telling you that in this life I have nothing that I desire except this, and God seems to give me to understand that this is the only means of my salvation, and of the salvation of many. I see well that this is such a grace that others than my merits would be needed to obtain it. But if that is what is sought, who is there that merits it? And then, if it is not otherwise owed to me, at least the continual petitions of so long a time must work something. I pray, and I conjure Your Paternity by all the saints of Paradise, that at last she bend to so many petitions, and to so many tears, and grant me in this her good pleasure — which I now offer to pay a thousand times over, by weight of blood. I finish by praying Your Paternity to grant me her benediction, and the license to run where God calls me."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most useless servant and son in Christ Jesus — Lonardo Cinami. Naples, 14 September 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, September 14, 1641",
  "date_only": "September 14, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 14,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 198,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2852"
 },
 "143": {
  "number": 143,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, September 15, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 174",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I write anew to Your Paternity, but I would that this letter were written in my own blood, so that it might the better and more readily obtain from Your Paternity the longed-for grace of going to the desired Indies. Our Father, I can say nothing else in this letter but that I am dying of desire, and I sigh the whole day long. O God, when shall I be consoled! For the love of the Lord, let Your Paternity look upon my grave faults (which have doubtless hindered me until now from receiving some fortunate news) immersed in the most precious Blood of Jesus Christ — and then exclude me, if he can. Let Your Paternity write to me what I must do to obtain what I ask of his charity with tears of blood, for I am ready to do all things, even to let my veins be opened and to be cut limb from limb. Believe me, Your Paternity, that I speak from the heart, and let the Crucifix before which I write this letter be my witness — oh, that I could speak to him face to face, to make him touch this truth with his hands! But I hope in the divine goodness and in the paternal charity of Your Paternity that I shall be consoled by being assigned as slave to those who shall go to China. I ask your holy blessing, and I commend myself to the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 15 September 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 15, 1641",
  "date_only": "September 15, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 15,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 199,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2763"
 },
 "144": {
  "number": 144,
  "byline_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi — Palermo, September 23, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 190",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I hear that some have already been notified for China — and what sentiments has the news of them not stirred within my soul! How I envy their lot! Fortunate, and a thousand times happy. Considering my grave sins, well worthy of a thousand hells, I would fear being excluded from so happy a lot, were it not that the most precious Blood of Christ and the Most Holy Virgin give me heart to aspire to so blessed a grace. Oh, that I were a bird, Our Father, so that flying I might come and declare to him by mouth the ardent desire I have to be assigned as slave to these true sons of the Company — for this is what I long for, as I have written so many times! I know for certain that Your Paternity, moved to pity for me, a poor wretch burning with desire, would grant me the longed-for grace for the desired China. But what then? Shall distance deny me what the goodness of the Lord and the charity of Your Paternity promise me? God forbid; I hope I shall be consoled, and with tears of blood I ask such consolation of Your Paternity. Yes, yes, Our Father, grant it to me for the love you bear the Most Holy Blood of Christ and the Most Holy Virgin, Saint Ignatius, Saint Francis Xavier, Blessed Aloysius, and his Guardian Angel; and since I have made a vow of it, as Your Paternity knows, deign to grant me its fulfillment, so that making of my whole self a perfect holocaust to the Lord, I may offer it entirely to Him, as I desire and long to do. O God, and how much? You know it — explain it yourself to Our Father. I ask your holy blessing, and I commend myself to the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy son — Luigi Spinelli. Palermo, 23 September 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Spinelli, Luigi",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, September 23, 1641",
  "date_only": "September 23, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 9,
  "day": 23,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 200,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2764"
 },
 "145": {
  "number": 145,
  "byline_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo — Naples, October 15, 1641",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 230",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I would not dare with others to do what I do with Your Paternity, but I know well to whom I write; and so, although the sentence seems to have been given, I appeal nonetheless to those bowels of a father which cannot but be moved, given their lovingkindness and benignity, at so many voices, so many groans, so many tears of a son of his so afflicted. That I should not go to the Indies? This is a sentence for me so painful that it cannot come (forgive me, Your Paternity, this word) from that father who loves me so, as I have known — having endured me in the Company for so long a time with so many failings. My Father, and why does Your Paternity not send me? The Father Procurator wants me and says there is a place for me; my Father Provincial does not hinder me. Only the will of Your Paternity has to be moved. She being so loving, and so benign, that there is no one who does not praise her: she gave me hope that when all the world were contrary to me, she alone should favor me; and now, whence comes it that this one alone opposes so many tears of mine? The last two letters of Your Paternity console me in some part. One tells me to continue knocking; another that she does not exclude me. Little remains after these premises to write me that Yes, which can send me to paradise. And I, kneeling before a crucifix, all tears, all sighs, and all sorrow, pray her, for the same Lord, that with her benediction she write it to me, while to the holy Sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity I commend myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most afflicted servant and son in Christ Jesus — Lonardo Cinami. Naples, 15 October 1641."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Cinami, Leonardo",
  "place_raw": "Naples",
  "date_raw": "Naples, October 15, 1641",
  "date_only": "October 15, 1641",
  "year": 1641,
  "month": 10,
  "day": 15,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 202,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2853"
 },
 "146": {
  "number": 146,
  "byline_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario — Caltanissetta, February 7, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 334",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "It matters greatly to me, for obtaining my longed-for Mission of Japan, or China, to clarify anew and more distinctly to Your Paternity how my vocation to the Indies has always been for Japan, or China, and not for the other parts; because I cannot believe that she, being informed of this truth, would abandon me in the melancholies in which I find myself. I, [Our] Father, conjure her charity (I have no more efficacious words to express my heart to her) to wish to aid a son of hers in Christ, who is bursting with melancholy — but with nothing else than to inform herself of the fact; and if she finds me a liar, I pray her to punish me most severely. I go mad thinking that the Lord has mortified me in having offered me the Philippines, for which I have declared I have no vocation; for, this having been pointed out to me by many, to remove the equivocation I have asked explicitly for Japan, or China, and so I have been accustomed to write to the Father Assistant of Portugal, and to Father Alvaro Semedo, Procurator of China; nor have I ever wished to write to the Father Procurator of the Philippines. Very many of the Province bear witness to this, chiefly my Master of Novices and the Masters of philosophy, as in the enclosed Your Paternity will be able to see; the same is seen in the enclosed formula of the [vow], which I offered in the secular year, when — having asked of Your Paternity leave to make a vow of it, and having had no reply on this point — I asked my Father Provincial to grant me license as far as his power could extend; which he doing for my consolation, I made the present formula, which many times a day I am accustomed to renew. Sorrow torments me too much; I do not know how to express my ardor to her — especially Your Paternity having added that she marveled that for the Philippines my sister made a difficulty, and not for Japan. I did not bring forward my sister as a difficulty for the Philippines; but to show Your Paternity that I am more than ever inflamed in my vocation for Japan, or China, I only said that, having had difficulty on account of my sister, yet to go to my longed-for Mission I was cutting away everything; and so de facto (in fact) I was about to dispose of it all, and indeed [it seems] the Lord had concurred abundantly in this, for then a vocation came to my sister to become a nun, and so I easily disentangled myself from everything. And already I was all ready to depart, although [hidden from me] the Call from Your Paternity were for the Philippines, or Japan and China — thinking I, and many others, that one Mission had been mistaken for the other — and in this form I wrote, taking leave of my Master of the Academy; but the Father Provincial having written to me anew that the Call was for the Philippines, [I drew back], and I held back the letter which I was to send to the said Father, which for my consolation Your Paternity will see.",
   "Our Father, have compassion on me, who am dying of pain; I consume myself thinking that the devil, for the punishment of my sins, must triumph [in] a material error — seeing that if Your Paternity changes [it], she will lift me from so many afflictions. I want to speak clearly, for the discharge of my conscience: Our Father, I am called by God ardently; nor do I leave [the scruple] to Your Paternity. Inform yourself well of the fact; and if you find that I am innocent, why will you permit that I lacerate myself with melancholy? I do not speak with exaggeration, for already some [nights] there assaults my heart a most [burning] sorrow, and they prognosticate for me some grave infirmity. I shall always live unhappily — this is [most true]; [and it comes] from poverty of spirit: that if I were well united with the Crucified, I ought to exult seeing myself mortified by God, and by my Superiors, in a thing so much at my heart, and without my fault.",
   "Too much have I wearied Your Paternity; forgive my ardor, and remember that I, with that to which she has done so great a charity — as is the calling me to the Philippines — my heart breaks; I have no words to show her my pain. The Most Blessed Virgin, from whom I know this vocation, will have compassion on me. This I pray Your Paternity: to send back to me the enclosed letters. And I commend myself to her in the Holy Sacrifices."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's most unworthy servant and son in Christ — Mario Finechiaro. Caltanissetta, 7 February 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "place_raw": "Caltanissetta",
  "date_raw": "Caltanissetta, February 7, 1642",
  "date_only": "February 7, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 2,
  "day": 7,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 203,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2759"
 },
 "147": {
  "number": 147,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, April 28, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 366",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "The Father Assistant of Spain in a reply of his tells me that the Father Procurator come hither from the West Indies does not want, nor will bring with him, subjects; and so for now there is no occasion to send other workmen — although he adds that another Father Procurator is being awaited, who will come for this purpose; whence he adds to me that on one, or the other occasion I shall be consoled. I wished to write it to Your Paternity so that by her all may be confirmed. Although my desire would be to be consoled as soon as possible, whence I pray and supplicate Your Paternity — for love of that Lord who gives me this desire, and to Your Paternity [a greater one], so that his glory be amplified through the world — that she wish to console me, and send me with the first. Our Father, I believe I would die of desire, and of holy envy, if I heard others depart and I remain. There is no doubt that I would know such to be the will of my Lord, but not for this would I not have occasion to weep for my many defects and imperfections, which have retarded me from so great a good. The great charity, and mercy of Your Paternity can cover, and forget these. I hope in the Lord that for the future I shall truly attend to holy mortification: although in many letters I have said this, and then I have not [done it], I commend myself to the prayers and Holy Sacrifices of Your Paternity, so that she obtain from the Lord grace perfectly to vanquish my passions, and to make me a worthy workman in his holy vineyard."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 28 April 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, April 28, 1642",
  "date_only": "April 28, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 4,
  "day": 28,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 206,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2824"
 },
 "148": {
  "number": 148,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, May 5, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 369",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
   "I am awaiting with very great anxiety some letter with which I may console myself, finding myself in that from Your Paternity assured of what I so desire. But my unworthiness has been cause that not only did I not obtain what I desire, but that I was not even esteemed worthy of a reply to so many of mine — for my consolation, so that from Your Paternity, as from the principal fount, I might know all. Yet I content myself, because I know all comes from him who is a most loving Father, whom I pray that for love of the Lord she wish to remember me in the occurrences. Your Paternity, believe me, that neither in the night do I dream of other, nor in the day think of other, than how I may be sent as soon as possible; but since for now no opportunity occurs, at least I pray and supplicate her that, when some opportunity occurs, I be included among those who shall go. Your Paternity forgive me if with continual letters I give her some annoyance, for I burn with desire, nor can I do otherwise; if she wants no more letters, send me this as soon as possible — or at least assure me, Your Paternity, that I shall be among the first — while to her most holy prayers and Sacrifices I commend myself."
  ],
  "signoff": [
   "Your Paternity's unworthy servant and son in the Lord — Giacomo Lazari. Palermo, 5 May 1642."
  ],
  "editorial_notes": [],
  "author_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo",
  "place_raw": "Palermo",
  "date_raw": "Palermo, May 5, 1642",
  "date_only": "May 5, 1642",
  "year": 1642,
  "month": 5,
  "day": 5,
  "date_note": null,
  "book_page_start": 208,
  "ijs_url": "https://indipetae.bc.edu/items/show/2825"
 },
 "149": {
  "number": 149,
  "byline_raw": "Lazari, Giacomo — Palermo, May 19, 1642",
  "language": "Italian",
  "archival_ref": "ARSI, FG 743, 370",
  "salutation": [
   "Most Reverend Father in Christ, Our Father",
   "Pax Christi"
  ],
  "body": [
